            <ArticleTitle>Opioids activate brain analgesic circuits through cytochrome P450/epoxygenase signaling.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>284-6</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1038/nn.2497</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>To assess the importance of brain cytochrome P450 (P450) activity in mu opioid analgesic action, we generated a mutant mouse with brain neuron-specific reductions in P450 activity; these mice showed highly attenuated morphine antinociception compared with controls. Pharmacological inhibition of brain P450 arachidonate epoxygenases also blocked morphine antinociception in mice and rats. Our findings indicate that a neuronal P450 epoxygenase mediates the pain-relieving properties of morphine.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
